WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.168
Chapter 11 is a worked case:
a large field-office estate,

2
00:00:04.168 --> 00:00:09.659
about 1,500 offices and 70,000 endpoints,
asked to fund a 50-million-dollar

3
00:00:09.659 --> 00:00:15.150
refresh of a Configuration Manager
distribution point in every office.

4
00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:19.588
Microsoft's published limit is 4,000
clients per distribution point.

5
00:00:19.588 --> 00:00:26.637
On capacity, the estate needs 24, two
at each of 12 hubs, for 96,000 clients.

6
00:00:26.637 --> 00:00:32.637
The other 1,476 exist only to
compensate for slow branch circuits.

7
00:00:32.637 --> 00:00:37.281
So fix the circuit: dedicated
internet access sized to seats,

8
00:00:37.281 --> 00:00:40.438
a 5G continuity path,
and an SD-WAN overlay.

9
00:00:40.438 --> 00:00:44.957
On the book's planning assumptions, the
network program needs about 10 million

10
00:00:44.957 --> 00:00:47.391
dollars of first-year capital and reaches

11
00:00:47.391 --> 00:00:49.963
a positive cumulative
position in year four.

12
00:00:49.963 --> 00:00:52.512
But the financial case
turns on one number.

13
00:00:52.512 --> 00:00:58.121
Breakeven is 873 dollars of current
MPLS cost per office per month;

14
00:00:58.121 --> 00:01:01.588
below that, argue capability, not savings.

15
00:01:01.588 --> 00:01:06.940
And no office's MPLS is retired until
its replacement telemetry is confirmed.
